WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.030
Just like any profession, the movie industry
is filled with a wide variety of personalities

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:09.910
that sometimes clash with one another — especially
when some of those personalities belong to

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.500
notoriously difficult performers.

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:16.600
Or, otherwise decent actors who get carried
away during the grueling process of making

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.760
big budget movies and television, earning
them a "does not play well with others" reputation.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:22.880
Actors like ...

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:24.520
William Shatner

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.720
Over the years, several of William Shatner's
former castmates have commented on the Star

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.260
Trek star's allegedly difficult attitude.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.760
He's reportedly upset plenty of co-stars and
even caused some feuds along the way.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:42.000
Shatner and fellow Trek vet Leonard Nimoy
clashed over scripts and fan mail, and Shatner

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.760
is said to be excessively sensitive to stories
that favored Nimoy.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.300
Later in life, the pair patched up their differences,
but that belated reconciliation may be a rare

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.520
exception for Shatner.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:59.000
Reportedly, Shatner and Nichelle Nichols—with
whom he shared TV's first interracial kiss—didn't

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:04.320
get along well at all, and she nearly left
the show until Dr. Martin Luther King convinced

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.100
Nichols of the importance of her role.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:11.700
Shatner later made waves when he refused to
appear on stage with James Doohan at a convention

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.480
event in 2004—Doohan's farewell appearance
following his Alzheimer's diagnosis.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:23.340
There's also the long-running feud between
George Takei and Shatner, who've been at odds—often

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:24.579
publicly—for several decades.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.680
"There's such a sickness there.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.580
It's so patently obvious that there's a psychosis
there."

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.359
"Well, I think his stability is quite questionable."

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:38.880
"Really?"

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.260
"Hahaha."

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:41.480
Shannen Doherty

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.020
Although she's managed to put her public feuds
with castmates behind her since her 2015 cancer

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:51.820
diagnosis, Shannen Doherty reportedly was
notoriously hard to get along with on the

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.480
sets of both Beverly Hills 90210 and Charmed.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:00.200
During her 90210 years, Doherty clashed with
her co-stars, including Tori Spelling, who

00:02:00.201 --> 00:02:04.179
recounted an incident when Doherty and Jennie
Garth nearly got into a fistfight.

00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:09.640
Spelling has admitted that she ultimately
asked her father, 90210 producer Aaron Spelling,

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:10.520
to fire Doherty.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.360
Years after the show ended, Jason Priestley
wrote candidly about Doherty's attitude in

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:20.040
his memoir, and Doherty responded by saying
he had "brain damage" from a car accident.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.740
On the set of Charmed, Doherty ran into problems
again, this time with co-star Alyssa Milano,

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.940
who described the situation as "very much
sort of like high school."

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.940
Doherty agreed ...

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:35.730
"There was too much drama on the set and not
enough passion for the work."

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.209
Whatever caused the issues, they proved to
be too much for Doherty, or producer Aaron

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:43.580
Spelling, to handle, and she left the series
after the third season.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:45.200
Wesley Snipes

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.200
Despite being a household name, Wesley Snipes
has developed a bad reputation in Hollywood

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:55.820
for his on-set antics, most notoriously during
the production of 2004's Blade: Trinity.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:01.540
According to co-star Patton Oswalt, Snipes
insisted on being called "Blade," and would

00:03:01.551 --> 00:03:02.850
rarely leave his trailer ...

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:08.640
"You'd walk by his trailer and this wall of,
of pot stench would just, like, woah, and

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.159
kind of push you to the side."

00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:16.250
Oswalt also recounted a much more serious
incident in which Snipes tried to strangle

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:21.349
the director, David Goyer, after which Goyer
proposed th at Snipes quit, and his stand-in

00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:22.260
finish the film.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.240
This allegedly freaked Snipes out so much
he stopped talking to Goyer altogether ...

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.689
"After a while he, he resorted to just, give,
he would communicate with, with, uh, Post-Its.

00:03:31.689 --> 00:03:36.469
He would give to the director, and each one
he would sign, 'Blade.'"

00:03:36.469 --> 00:03:41.969
After filming for Blade: Trinity ended, Snipes
sued Goyer and New Line Cinema, claiming they

00:03:41.969 --> 00:03:46.410
withheld part of his salary and didn't allow
him to be part of the creative process.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:50.700
He was also allegedly upset because some of
his screen time had been reduced in favor

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.739
of Jessica Biel and Ryan Reynolds.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.880
The suit was later settled out of court.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:56.320
Lindsay Lohan

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:01.420
After racking up a string of early hits as
a Disney starlet, Lindsay Lohan set about

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:06.790
compiling a record of partying hard and leaving
frustrated directors and co-stars in her wake.

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:13.659
On the set of 2007's Georgia Rule, Lohan caused
so many problems with her tardiness and no-shows—reportedly

00:04:13.659 --> 00:04:17.850
costing the production a half a million dollars
a day—that producer James Robinson sent

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.350
her an ultimatum, saying, quote, "I'm not
going to put up with it."

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:26.560
In 2012, a source from the set of Glee complained
that the actress had been "a total nightmare,"

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.300
again showing up several hours late to shoot.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.080
It seems that tardiness is a bad habit of
Lohan's.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.420
A year later, Anger Management co-star Charlie
Sheen told Jay Leno that Lohan had been late

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:41.920
to the set by several hours, and had later
"borrowed" a couple of bracelets from the

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:45.040
production, which the show later deducted
from her paycheck …

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.200
The same year, New York Times Magazine published
"Here Is What Happens When You Cast Lindsay

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:56.350
Lohan in Your Movie," a piece chronicling
some of Lohan's almost unbelievable misadventures

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.840
on the set of Paul Schrader's The Canyons.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.060
Among her offenses?

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:00.960
Not showing up to set.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:02.000
Big surprise.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:03.580
Edward Norton

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:08.460
Though he's apparently become more easygoing
in recent years, it's been a hard battle to

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.600
repair the damage Ed Norton caused to his
reputation in the past.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.020
That reputation?

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.260
A supposedly impossible perfectionist who
siphons creative control for himself.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:23.210
While Norton is infamous for insisting that
producers give him say over the final cut

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:30.100
of the 1998 drama American History X, he surged
back into the headlines while promoting 2008's

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:31.040
The Incredible Hulk.

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:36.100
The Marvel Cinematic Universe is generally
action-packed, explosive, and witty.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:40.930
But when Edward Norton signed on to play Bruce
Banner, he wanted a longer, more nuanced film

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.040
with a deeper dive into Banner's backstory.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.240
Universal put its foot down, and the "creative
differences" spiraled out of control.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.990
Norton barely even promoted the film, and
took off for a humanitarian trip to Africa

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:57.330
after the premiere, arguably harming the flick's
box office bottom line.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.980
So in other words, you wouldn't like Norton
when he's angry …

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:01.700
roar

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:06.640
Thanks for watching!

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.740
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
